tk-drm-upstream: Linux DRM core type definitions and ports (x86_64, v7.2.3)
==========================================================================

Layer: platform. Depends only on tk-linux-kapi (kernel-API shims).

Type modules (`*_types.rs`) are field-for-field mirrors of the C definitions
in the Linux 7.2.3 headers named in each module's header comment (MIT-licensed
data layouts; Linux headers are GPL-2.0-only and are cited, not copied).
Layouts were generated from the DWARF of the configured Linux oracle
(config/linux/7.2.3-q35-graphics.config, THP enabled) and every size and field
offset is pinned by a compile-time assertion.

Function ports (`*_upstream.rs`) mirror the Linux `.c` files named in their
header comments. Each stub is filled by its owning batch (see the module
header comment).

Linux sources are licensed per file, and each translation follows the license of
its own source:

- MIT or MIT-compatible X11-style permission (no SPDX line, or `MIT`, or
  `GPL-2.0 OR MIT` with the MIT option taken): drm_blend, drm_modes, drm_fourcc,
  drm_framebuffer, drm_rect, drm_print, drm_displayid, drm_eld, drm_modeset_lock,
  drm_modeset_helper, drm_color_mgmt, drm_damage_helper (GPL-2.0 OR MIT, MIT option),
  drm_vblank, drm_vblank_work, drm_plane_helper, drm_plane, drm_crtc, drm_connector,
  drm_encoder, drm_mode_config, drm_mode_object, drm_property, drm_edid,
  drivers/video/hdmi.c, drm_scdc_helper, drm_hdmi_helper, and
  drm_hdmi_state_helper. Copyright lines are those of each Linux file.
- GPL-2.0 only, NOT covered by the MIT grant: `drivers/gpu/drm/drm_managed.c`
  (translated in `drm_managed_upstream.rs`). Its translation is flagged for
  rework before this crate ships; it must not be relied on as MIT until resolved.
- GPL-2.0-or-later, no code translated yet: `drm_gem_atomic_helper.c` and
  `drm_gem_framebuffer_helper.c` (the `*_upstream.rs` files are placeholders).

The Linux sources are not verbatim copies of the C text except where a line is
marked `// upstream:`; these files are MIT translations of structure and behaviour.

Test fixtures: every EDID used by the host tests is built in code
(`drm_edid_upstream.rs`, `tests` module). No Linux test data is copied.

Pending-stub definitions (`src/pending_stubs/`) are temporary, owned by later
batches, and each names its replacing Linux file in its module header.
